Z||000000||nivlek68||08-05-2000||10:49 AM||||i been kicking around an idea for awhile now. move the battery under the spare,lower the canastor on the driverside down a little. then install 9 inch pusher fans on the grills,with a thermostat switch mounted to an oil line,or oil pan. the switch i was looking at turned on at 170 and off at 150,what do you guys and gals think about this. it would help to cool off the engine compartment.||||reg||3
Z||000001||deceler8||08-05-2000||02:33 PM||||I solved this problem by getting two decklid vents at the boneyard ($1 apiece) and removing the baffles on the underside. <P>They are held on by rivets which drill out easily. Some use speed clips, which are even easier to remove, and you can reattach the baffles. I then stripped, painted, and clear coated them and they look great. They pass a lot more air, and give a peek of my shiny engine to boot. <P>And I kept my baffled vents for the fall/winter. <P>Might be a cheap alternative to the pusher fan idea.||||reg||1
Z||000002||AL||08-05-2000||03:35 PM||||i saw a fiero with 2 fans on the vents once but it didn't Look to good, now if you do this from the inside you might get away with moving the battery but the other side would be tricky, i thInk on the battery side it would work with 1 fan  from under the vent, you hit the switch and it would move oUt hot air quicker, try it keep us posted,AL.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||08-05-2000||06:22 PM||||I have also thought about doing the fans under the grills. I already removed the leaf traps, but more air flow would have to be better. The only problem so far- when running at low speeds with the air running, the battery voltage drops below normal (verified by meter on battery). I have a 94 amp alternator, and cant afford to buy another higher output model yet.||||reg||1
Z||000004||WKDFIRO||08-06-2000||03:28 PM||||Engine heat is going to be a way of life on a mid engine car. Putting fans on the vents could very well be blocking more air flow than helping it. Especially if your fan is blowing IN to the engine bay rather than trying to draw it out the way it was intended. <BR>     As far as cooling ideas, I have a manual fan switch, which activates the engine fans (at coil and alternator) and my radiator fan. It is an auxilary switch using already existing wiring so the original system is intact. (Green wire with white stripe, find a way to ground it and you have a manual fan switch. Simple!) I have also take the vents and opened them up. Between that and the fan switch the car runs great, I don't worry when I sit in traffic and Im not trying to reinvent the wheel! <BR>    Other people have gone the route of a larger radiator (a 3 core I beleive) for the cost of repairing a plastic tank on an old one. (Im trying to remember where it was...)But that is a matter of preference. <BR>    I just never felt comfortable putting anything on the vents. But thats just me. <BR>  Hope this helps.||||reg||7
Z||000005||nivlek68||08-06-2000||04:57 PM||||pusher fan moves air out of engine compartment not in,if i wanted to pull air in i put the over the foor scopes (bad idea).cutting wires bad idea, plan to hook in somewhere(like the green wire with white stripe thanks for the info there)    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||DaRkLoRD||08-06-2000||06:25 PM||||Several months ago I was talking to a forum member at a Fiero meet, and he mentioned the same thing. I thought about it for a while, and then suggested he look into radiator fans, as they're already suited to engine temperatures, as well as possibly water running down over them.<P>how do these compare to the fans you have in mind?<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A> <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000007||nivlek68||08-06-2000||07:16 PM||||those are the ones i was talking about.the ones i seen in the jc whitney catalog, they are less then 3 inches thick the only problem i see is the diameter of the fan and shroud is 10-1/4 inches.||||reg||1
